1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:04,257
Behind every wall socket there is a pair of wires.

2
00:00:04,587 --> 00:00:07,897
And in those wires, something odd is going on.

3
00:00:08,027 --> 00:00:11,127
The current is not flowing out to your lamp and back,

4
00:00:11,147 --> 00:00:13,777
round and round, like water in a pipe.

5
00:00:14,107 --> 00:00:15,377
It flows one way.

6
00:00:15,707 --> 00:00:16,897
Then it stops.

7
00:00:16,987 --> 00:00:18,417
Then it flows the other way.

8
00:00:18,747 --> 00:00:20,177
Then it stops again.

9
00:00:20,347 --> 00:00:24,167
In most of the world, it changes direction a hundred times

10
00:00:24,187 --> 00:00:25,297
every second.

11
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:28,977
This is alternating current, or AC.

12
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:32,247
It is the kind of electricity that every power station

13
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:34,657
makes and every home receives.

14
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:37,137
It raises some fair questions.

15
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:39,367
If the current keeps reversing,

16
00:00:39,387 --> 00:00:40,977
how does anything get done?

17
00:00:41,387 --> 00:00:43,767
If the voltage is always changing,

18
00:00:43,787 --> 00:00:46,447
what does it mean to say the supply is two hundred and

19
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:47,847
thirty volts?

20
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:51,217
And why did the world choose something this strange?

21
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:54,407
In this series we take AC apart,

22
00:00:54,427 --> 00:00:56,327
one idea at a time.

23
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:58,177
We start with its shape.

24
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:03,033
First, what are the electrons actually doing?

25
00:01:03,053 --> 00:01:06,473
In the very first video, we saw that electrons in a wire

26
00:01:06,493 --> 00:01:11,153
drift astonishingly slowly, less than a millimetre a second.

27
00:01:11,173 --> 00:01:14,633
With alternating current they do not even get that far.

28
00:01:14,653 --> 00:01:16,793
They move a tiny distance one way,

29
00:01:16,813 --> 00:01:18,953
and then the same distance back.

30
00:01:18,973 --> 00:01:22,233
In an ordinary household wire, the whole journey is a

31
00:01:22,253 --> 00:01:24,353
few thousandths of a millimetre.

32
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:27,393
The electrons in your lamp's cable have been in that cable

33
00:01:27,413 --> 00:01:29,353
since the day it was made.

34
00:01:29,373 --> 00:01:31,833
So how does the energy arrive?

35
00:01:31,853 --> 00:01:33,433
Think of a handsaw.

36
00:01:33,453 --> 00:01:35,843
The blade goes forward and back,

37
00:01:35,973 --> 00:01:37,273
forward and back.

38
00:01:37,293 --> 00:01:39,086
It never travels anywhere.

39
00:01:39,106 --> 00:01:42,926
But it cuts on every stroke, and the work gets done.

40
00:01:42,946 --> 00:01:45,993
Or think of rocking a long chain back and forth at one

41
00:01:46,013 --> 00:01:46,713
end.

42
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:49,433
The far end rocks too, at once.

43
00:01:49,453 --> 00:01:51,673
No link travels the length of the chain,

44
00:01:51,693 --> 00:01:53,833
and yet your effort arrives.

45
00:01:53,853 --> 00:01:55,673
It is the same in the wire.

46
00:01:55,693 --> 00:01:58,740
The electrons at the power station push on their neighbours,

47
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:01,593
which push on theirs, all the way to your lamp.

48
00:02:01,613 --> 00:02:03,780
What travels is the push.

49
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,793
And a lamp filament does not care which way the electrons

50
00:02:06,813 --> 00:02:07,993
in it are moving.

51
00:02:08,013 --> 00:02:10,046
Either way they collide with the atoms,

52
00:02:10,066 --> 00:02:11,763
and the filament gets hot.

53
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:15,273
To talk about alternating current,

54
00:02:15,293 --> 00:02:16,483
we draw it.

55
00:02:16,653 --> 00:02:20,323
Time runs along, and the voltage goes up and down.

56
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:23,403
You already know where this shape comes from.

57
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:27,113
At the end of the last series we saw a generator:

58
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:30,553
a loop of wire turning in a magnetic field.

59
00:02:30,573 --> 00:02:33,913
As it turns, the voltage rises to a peak,

60
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:37,193
falls through zero, swings to a peak the other way,

61
00:02:37,213 --> 00:02:38,713
and comes back.

62
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:40,993
Watch one point on the turning loop,

63
00:02:41,013 --> 00:02:43,753
and plot its height as time goes by.

64
00:02:43,773 --> 00:02:45,593
Out comes the wave.

65
00:02:45,613 --> 00:02:49,673
One complete turn of the loop draws one complete wave.

66
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:51,963
We call that one cycle.

67
00:02:52,093 --> 00:02:53,763
Three words describe it.

68
00:02:53,853 --> 00:02:56,793
The time for one cycle is the period.

69
00:02:56,813 --> 00:03:00,513
The number of cycles in one second is the frequency.

70
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:04,113
Its unit is the hertz, named after Heinrich Hertz,

71
00:03:04,133 --> 00:03:07,283
whom we met when he proved that radio waves exist.

72
00:03:07,453 --> 00:03:10,403
One hertz is one cycle each second.

73
00:03:10,733 --> 00:03:14,483
In most of the world, the mains frequency is fifty hertz.

74
00:03:14,573 --> 00:03:18,633
Fifty cycles a second means each cycle lasts one fiftieth

75
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:21,243
of a second: twenty thousandths.

76
00:03:21,293 --> 00:03:23,913
And because the current changes direction twice in each

77
00:03:23,933 --> 00:03:27,283
cycle, it reverses a hundred times a second.

78
00:03:27,693 --> 00:03:31,513
Period and frequency are two ways of saying the same thing.

79
00:03:31,533 --> 00:03:34,403
Divide one into one, and you get the other.

80
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:38,717
The curve has a name: a sine wave.

81
00:03:39,047 --> 00:03:42,227
And it is worth asking why the mains should have this

82
00:03:42,247 --> 00:03:43,277
particular shape.

83
00:03:43,607 --> 00:03:46,907
Why not a square wave, switching sharply from one direction

84
00:03:46,927 --> 00:03:47,837
to the other?

85
00:03:47,927 --> 00:03:49,197
Or a zigzag?

86
00:03:49,367 --> 00:03:50,877
One reason we have seen.

87
00:03:51,207 --> 00:03:53,667
It is what you get from steady rotation,

88
00:03:53,687 --> 00:03:55,357
and generators rotate.

89
00:03:55,767 --> 00:03:59,187
But there is a deeper reason, and it is why engineers

90
00:03:59,207 --> 00:04:00,637
love this curve.

91
00:04:00,807 --> 00:04:03,957
Recall what a coil and a capacitor respond to.

92
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:07,907
A coil produces a voltage that depends on how fast the

93
00:04:07,927 --> 00:04:09,387
current is changing.

94
00:04:09,407 --> 00:04:12,867
A capacitor passes a current that depends on how fast

95
00:04:12,887 --> 00:04:14,787
the voltage is changing.

96
00:04:14,807 --> 00:04:17,347
Both care about the rate of change.

97
00:04:17,367 --> 00:04:21,107
Now take a sine wave and ask how fast it is changing at

98
00:04:21,127 --> 00:04:22,347
each moment.

99
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:25,587
Plot the answer, and you get another sine wave.

100
00:04:25,607 --> 00:04:29,667
Same shape, same frequency, only shifted along.

101
00:04:29,687 --> 00:04:32,117
No other repeating shape does this.

102
00:04:32,247 --> 00:04:35,827
Put a square wave into a circuit with coils and capacitors,

103
00:04:35,847 --> 00:04:39,797
and it comes out distorted, with spikes and rounded corners.

104
00:04:39,847 --> 00:04:42,917
Put a sine wave in, and a sine wave comes out.

105
00:04:42,967 --> 00:04:45,997
Its size may change, and it may be shifted in time.

106
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:48,237
But its shape survives.

107
00:04:48,327 --> 00:04:52,067
That is why a power system built on sine waves stays tidy

108
00:04:52,087 --> 00:04:54,077
from the generator to your socket.

109
00:04:54,407 --> 00:04:57,907
It is also the natural shape of anything that swings.

110
00:04:57,927 --> 00:05:00,227
A pendulum, a weight on a spring,

111
00:05:00,247 --> 00:05:03,747
a child on a swing: plot any of them against time,

112
00:05:03,767 --> 00:05:05,837
and you get this same curve.

113
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:08,550
Now for size.

114
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:12,140
The height of the wave, from zero to its crest,

115
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:15,430
is called the peak value, or the amplitude.

116
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:17,110
Here is a surprise.

117
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:20,150
Put a meter on a wall socket in Europe,

118
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:23,460
India or Australia, and it reads about two hundred and

119
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:24,550
thirty volts.

120
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:27,980
But the voltage in that socket does not peak at two hundred

121
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:29,100
and thirty.

122
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,340
Twice in every cycle, it reaches three hundred and twenty-five.

123
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:36,580
In North America, the meter says one hundred and twenty,

124
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:39,220
and the peak is one hundred and seventy.

125
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:42,870
So the number printed on every appliance is not the peak.

126
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:45,830
It is not the average either, as we are about to see.

127
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:47,580
It is a third thing.

128
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,780
And to understand it, we have to ask what the number is

129
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,670
for.

130
00:05:53,033 --> 00:05:56,293
What single number should we use to describe a voltage

131
00:05:56,313 --> 00:05:58,183
that never stands still?

132
00:05:58,313 --> 00:06:00,383
The obvious choice is the average.

133
00:06:00,713 --> 00:06:01,343
But look.

134
00:06:01,673 --> 00:06:05,573
The wave spends exactly as much time below zero as above

135
00:06:05,593 --> 00:06:05,903
it.

136
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:07,903
Its average is zero.

137
00:06:08,233 --> 00:06:09,343
That cannot be right.

138
00:06:09,673 --> 00:06:13,023
A voltage whose average is zero still boils a kettle.

139
00:06:13,513 --> 00:06:15,183
So try a different test.

140
00:06:15,513 --> 00:06:17,663
Do not ask what the voltage is.

141
00:06:17,913 --> 00:06:19,943
Ask what it can do.

142
00:06:20,153 --> 00:06:22,143
Take two identical heaters.

143
00:06:22,393 --> 00:06:24,543
Run one from alternating current.

144
00:06:24,873 --> 00:06:27,733
Run the other from a steady, direct voltage,

145
00:06:27,753 --> 00:06:31,573
like a battery, and adjust that voltage until both heaters

146
00:06:31,593 --> 00:06:34,383
give out heat at exactly the same rate.

147
00:06:34,553 --> 00:06:37,653
Whatever steady voltage does that is the fair measure

148
00:06:37,673 --> 00:06:39,253
of the alternating one.

149
00:06:39,273 --> 00:06:41,613
We call it the effective value.

150
00:06:41,633 --> 00:06:43,013
We can work it out.

151
00:06:43,033 --> 00:06:46,373
The heat in a resistor depends on the voltage squared.

152
00:06:46,393 --> 00:06:48,293
So square the wave.

153
00:06:48,313 --> 00:06:50,933
Squaring makes every part of it positive,

154
00:06:50,953 --> 00:06:52,933
the dips as well as the crests,

155
00:06:52,953 --> 00:06:56,453
which is exactly right, because a heater warms up whichever

156
00:06:56,473 --> 00:06:58,133
way the current flows.

157
00:06:58,153 --> 00:07:00,533
Now take the average, the mean,

158
00:07:00,553 --> 00:07:02,253
of that squared wave.

159
00:07:02,273 --> 00:07:05,183
Then take the square root, to undo the squaring.

160
00:07:05,273 --> 00:07:07,943
Root, of the mean, of the square.

161
00:07:08,073 --> 00:07:09,853
That is where the name comes from:

162
00:07:09,873 --> 00:07:11,423
the R M S value.

163
00:07:11,513 --> 00:07:14,623
For a sine wave, it always comes out the same way.

164
00:07:14,873 --> 00:07:18,533
The RMS value is the peak divided by the square root of

165
00:07:18,553 --> 00:07:21,023
two: about seventy percent of the peak.

166
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:24,703
So two hundred and thirty volts is an RMS value.

167
00:07:24,873 --> 00:07:29,333
It means: this supply heats a resistor exactly as a steady

168
00:07:29,353 --> 00:07:31,343
two hundred and thirty volts would.

169
00:07:31,433 --> 00:07:34,533
When an engineer quotes an alternating voltage or current,

170
00:07:34,553 --> 00:07:37,903
and says nothing else, RMS is what they mean.

171
00:07:37,993 --> 00:07:40,623
But remember the peak is still there.

172
00:07:40,713 --> 00:07:43,813
Insulation has to withstand three hundred and twenty-five

173
00:07:43,833 --> 00:07:45,983
volts, not two hundred and thirty.

174
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:48,943
Why fifty hertz?

175
00:07:49,193 --> 00:07:51,023
Or, if you live in the Americas,

176
00:07:51,193 --> 00:07:52,533
why sixty?

177
00:07:52,553 --> 00:07:55,183
There is no deep law of nature behind it.

178
00:07:55,273 --> 00:07:58,463
In the early days, every company picked its own.

179
00:07:58,553 --> 00:08:01,173
There were systems at sixteen and two thirds,

180
00:08:01,193 --> 00:08:04,733
at twenty-five, forty, fifty, sixty,

181
00:08:04,753 --> 00:08:07,263
and a hundred and thirty-three cycles a second.

182
00:08:07,673 --> 00:08:09,663
The choice is a compromise.

183
00:08:09,753 --> 00:08:12,613
Go too low, and lamps flicker visibly,

184
00:08:12,633 --> 00:08:15,383
and transformers have to be large and heavy.

185
00:08:15,513 --> 00:08:19,343
Go too high, and the losses in lines and machines climb.

186
00:08:19,753 --> 00:08:22,863
Somewhere between forty and sixty is comfortable.

187
00:08:23,273 --> 00:08:27,013
In the United States, Westinghouse settled on sixty.

188
00:08:27,033 --> 00:08:30,493
In Germany, the firm of AEG chose fifty,

189
00:08:30,513 --> 00:08:32,783
a rounder number in a metric country.

190
00:08:32,953 --> 00:08:35,253
Each sold its equipment to its neighbours,

191
00:08:35,273 --> 00:08:37,503
and the world divided between them.

192
00:08:37,673 --> 00:08:40,063
One country still shows the join.

193
00:08:40,233 --> 00:08:43,653
In eighteen ninety-five, Tokyo bought its generators from

194
00:08:43,673 --> 00:08:45,783
AEG, at fifty hertz.

195
00:08:45,833 --> 00:08:49,093
A year later, Osaka bought from General Electric,

196
00:08:49,113 --> 00:08:50,373
at sixty.

197
00:08:50,393 --> 00:08:53,333
Japan has run on two frequencies ever since,

198
00:08:53,353 --> 00:08:56,613
and the two halves of its grid can exchange power only

199
00:08:56,633 --> 00:08:58,863
through special converter stations.

200
00:08:59,033 --> 00:09:01,503
Other choices survive where they suit the job.

201
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:04,623
Some railways still use sixteen and two thirds.

202
00:09:04,873 --> 00:09:07,013
Aircraft use four hundred hertz,

203
00:09:07,033 --> 00:09:10,333
because at a high frequency transformers and motors can

204
00:09:10,353 --> 00:09:14,453
be made small and light, and on an aircraft every kilogram

205
00:09:14,473 --> 00:09:15,343
counts.

206
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,440
There is one more thing a sine wave has,

207
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:21,730
besides its size and its frequency.

208
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:23,490
It has timing.

209
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:26,370
Take two waves of the same frequency.

210
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:29,040
If they rise and fall together,

211
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:31,130
we say they are in phase.

212
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:33,960
But one may run a little behind the other,

213
00:09:33,980 --> 00:09:36,250
reaching its crest a moment later.

214
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:38,530
Then they are out of phase.

215
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:41,040
We measure the gap not in seconds,

216
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:42,850
but as a fraction of a turn.

217
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:44,770
Think of the generator again.

218
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:47,320
Fix two loops on the same shaft,

219
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:48,770
at an angle to each other.

220
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:52,160
They produce two waves, identical in shape,

221
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:55,530
with one lagging the other by exactly that angle.

222
00:09:55,660 --> 00:09:58,050
A quarter of a turn is ninety degrees.

223
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,520
Half a turn is a hundred and eighty,

224
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:03,730
and then one wave is the mirror image of the other.

225
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:07,880
This idea of phase will turn out to be the key to everything

226
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:08,930
that follows.

227
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:12,450
It decides how much power a circuit really takes.

228
00:10:12,540 --> 00:10:15,400
It is the secret of the three-phase system that carries

229
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,520
power across every country.

230
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:20,850
And it starts with the simplest question in AC.

231
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:23,990
So let's put it together.

232
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:27,710
Alternating current flows one way and then the other.

233
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:30,420
The electrons only shuffle back and forth,

234
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:32,590
but the energy still arrives.

235
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:35,110
One complete swing is a cycle.

236
00:10:35,160 --> 00:10:37,340
The time it takes is the period,

237
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:40,820
and the number of cycles each second is the frequency,

238
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:41,630
in hertz.

239
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:45,620
The shape is a sine wave, because it comes from rotation,

240
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:49,140
and because it is the one shape that coils and capacitors

241
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:50,350
do not distort.

242
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:52,870
The height of the wave is its peak.

243
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:55,780
The number on the label is the RMS value,

244
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:58,740
the steady voltage that would give the same heat:

245
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:00,710
about seventy percent of the peak.

246
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:04,260
And two waves of the same frequency can be in step,

247
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:07,710
or out of step, by an angle we call phase.

248
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:10,710
Next time, we ask that simplest question.

249
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:13,380
Put a resistor on an alternating supply,

250
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:15,590
then a coil, then a capacitor.

251
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:18,820
In two of the three, the current refuses to keep time

252
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:20,020
with the voltage.

253
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,270
We will see why.

254
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:24,740
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

255
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:27,790
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
